 \section{Limits from scratch}
+
+For a real number $x$, define $|x|=\max\{x,-x\}$. Thus
+$-|x|\le x\le|x|$, and $|x|\le r$ means $-r\le x\le r$ when $r\ge0$.
+We will use two elementary estimates repeatedly.
+
+\begin{proposition}[real triangle inequalities]\label{prop:real-triangle}
+For real $a,b$,
+\[
+|a+b|\le|a|+|b|,\qquad \bigl||a|-|b|\bigr|\le|a-b|.
+\]
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Adding $-|a|\le a\le|a|$ and $-|b|\le b\le|b|$ gives
+$-(|a|+|b|)\le a+b\le|a|+|b|$, which is the first inequality.
+Apply it to $a=(a-b)+b$: $|a|-|b|\le|a-b|$.
+Swapping $a,b$ gives $|b|-|a|\le|a-b|$; together these give the second.
+\end{proof}
 
