+\section{Continuity and derivatives, from completeness}
+
+The preface promised that every fact this volume uses would be proved from the axioms, with a short list of exceptions. This section shortens the
+list. Five theorems about functions on a segment are used throughout the series, often without a word: a continuous function that changes sign
+vanishes somewhere, it is bounded and attains its bounds, it is uniformly continuous, and a differentiable function that takes the same value twice
+has a horizontal tangent in between, which gives the mean value theorem. All five come out of Proposition~\ref{prop:seqcrit}, that is, out of the
+completeness of~$\R$, in about two pages.
+
+\begin{reminder}[continuity and derivatives]
+Let $f$ be defined on an interval $I$, and let $a$ be an accumulation point of
+$I$: every neighbourhood of $a$ contains a point of $I$ different from $a$.
+We say $f(x)\to L$ as $x\to a$ if for every $\varepsilon>0$ there is $\delta>0$ such that
+$|f(x)-L|\le\varepsilon$ whenever $x\in I$ and $0<|x-a|\le\delta$. When $a\in I$, the function $f$ is \emph{continuous at $a$} if
+$f(x)\to f(a)$ as $x\to a$, and
+\emph{continuous on $I$} if it is continuous at every point of $I$. At an interior point $a$ of $I$, it is \emph{differentiable at $a$} if $\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}$ has a limit as
+$x\to a$, called $f'(a)$. At endpoints we use a one-sided derivative only when explicitly
+stated. On a singleton interval every function is continuous by convention,
+but no derivative is defined there. A differentiable function is continuous ($f(x)-f(a)=\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}(x-a)\to f'(a)\cdot0$).
+
+\emph{Sequences see everything.} $f$ is continuous at $a$ if and only if $f(x_n)\to f(a)$ for every sequence $x_n\to a$ in $I$. Indeed, if $f$ is continuous
+at $a$ and $x_n\to a$, then given $\varepsilon$ take the $\delta$ of continuity and then $N$ with $|x_n-a|\le\delta$ for $n\ge N$: for such $n$,
+$|f(x_n)-f(a)|\le\varepsilon$. Conversely, if $f$ is not continuous at $a$, some $\varepsilon$ defeats every $\delta$: for $\delta=\frac1n$ there is $x_n$
+with $|x_n-a|\le\frac1n$ and $|f(x_n)-f(a)|>\varepsilon$, so $x_n\to a$ but $f(x_n)\not\to f(a)$. The same argument works for every limit statement, and
+it is the bridge between the theorems about sequences above and the theorems about functions below.
+
+Sums, products and quotients (where the denominator does not vanish) of continuous functions are continuous, and so are composites: this is the
+proposition on operations on limits, read through sequences. Polynomials are continuous; so are $\exp$, $\ln$, $\cos$ and $\sin$, whose basic
+properties the volume admits.
+\end{reminder}
+
+\begin{proposition}[elementary differentiation rules]\label{prop:early-differentiation}
+Let $f,g$ be differentiable at an interior point $a$ of their intervals of
+definition, and let $\lambda,\mu\in\R$.
+\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
+\item $(\lambda f+\mu g)'(a)=\lambda f'(a)+\mu g'(a)$ and
+$(fg)'(a)=f'(a)g(a)+f(a)g'(a)$. If $g(a)\ne0$, then
+\[
+\left(\frac fg\right)'(a)=\frac{f'(a)g(a)-f(a)g'(a)}{g(a)^2}.
+\]
+\item If $h$ is differentiable at $f(a)$ and $h\circ f$ is defined near $a$,
+then $(h\circ f)'(a)=h'(f(a))f'(a)$.
+\end{enumerate}
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+(a) Linearity follows by taking limits of the difference quotients. For the product, add and subtract $f(a)g(x)$:
+\[
+\frac{f(x)g(x)-f(a)g(a)}{x-a}=\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}\,g(x)+f(a)\,\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}.
+\]
+Here $g(x)\to g(a)$ since $g$ is continuous at $a$. For the quotient it suffices to differentiate $\frac1g$:
+\[
+\frac1{x-a}\left(\frac1{g(x)}-\frac1{g(a)}\right)=-\frac{g(x)-g(a)}{x-a}\cdot\frac1{g(x)g(a)}\to-\frac{g'(a)}{g(a)^2}.
+\]
+Then apply the product rule to $f\cdot\frac1g$.
+
+(b) Write $h(y)-h(b)=(y-b)\bigl(h'(b)+\eta(y)\bigr)$ with $b=f(a)$ and $\eta(y)\to0$ as $y\to b$, $\eta(b)=0$; this is just the definition of $h'(b)$, rewritten so that it also makes sense when $y=b$. Put $y=f(x)$:
+$\frac{h(f(x))-h(f(a))}{x-a}=\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a}\bigl(h'(b)+\eta(f(x))\bigr)$, and as $x\to a$, $f(x)\to b$ by continuity, so $\eta(f(x))\to0$ and the right side tends to $f'(a)h'(b)$. (The rewriting avoids dividing by $f(x)-f(a)$, which may vanish for $x$ near $a$.)
+\end{proof}
+
+For example, define $x^\alpha=\exp(\alpha\ln x)$ for $x>0$ and real $\alpha$.
+Using the named elementary-function derivatives $(\exp)'=\exp$ and
+$\ln'(x)=1/x$, the chain rule gives
+\[
+\frac{\mathrm d}{\mathrm dx}x^\alpha
+=\exp(\alpha\ln x)\frac\alpha x=\alpha x^{\alpha-1}.
+\]
+Thus the differentiation used in the series comparisons below follows from
+proved rules and the stated elementary-function assumptions.
+
