+\begin{proposition}[when changing the order is safe]\label{prop:safe-sums}
+For $a_{ij}\ge0$, the two iterated sums $\sum_i\sum_j a_{ij}$ and
+$\sum_j\sum_i a_{ij}$ are equal, allowing $+\infty$.
+An absolutely convergent real series retains its sum under any permutation
+of its terms.
+\end{proposition}
+\begin{proof}
+Let $M$ be the supremum of all finite rectangle sums
+$\sum_{i=0}^m\sum_{j=0}^n a_{ij}$, with $M=+\infty$ when they are unbounded.
+For fixed $m$, letting $n$ increase gives $\sum_{i=0}^m\sum_j a_{ij}\le M$;
+letting $m$ increase gives the first iterated sum at most $M$.
+Every finite rectangle sum is also at most that iterated sum, so equality holds.
+The same argument in the other order gives the second equality.
+
+For absolute rearrangement, write $S=\sum_{k\ge0}u_k$. Given $\varepsilon>0$,
+choose $K$ with $\sum_{k>K}|u_k|<\varepsilon$. For a permutation $\sigma$,
+choose $N$ so that $\sigma(0),\dots,\sigma(N)$ include $0,\dots,K$.
+Any later rearranged partial sum contains these first $K+1$ terms; its difference
+from $S$ is a sum of omitted terms with original indices greater than $K$,
+and has absolute value at most $\varepsilon$. Hence it tends to $S$.
+\end{proof}
